#v(0.8em)

In the last place the duties of masters are declared by the Apostle, and that in this verse, where he noteth,

- 1\. The #emph[kinds] of their duties.
- 2\. A #emph[reason] to enforce the same.

The #emph[kinds] are set forth

+ Generally #emph[(do the same things.)]
+ Particularly #emph[(forbearing threatening.)]

The #emph[reason] is taken from that subjection wherein masters are to an higher master #emph[(knowing that your master also.)] And it is amplified,

- 1\. By the equal subjection of masters and servants to that master #emph[(your also.)]
- 2\. By the place where that master is #emph[(in heaven.)]
- 3\. By his property, in this phrase #emph[(neither is there respect of persons with him.)]

Because the several duties of masters are here but pointed at, and infolded under general terms, I will (as in former treatises hath been done) propound a distinct order, whereby we may the better find out their duties, and handle them one after another.

All may be drawn unto these two heads.

- 1\. Care in choosing good servants.
- 2\. Conscience in well using them.

For well using their servants, masters must have an eye to their place, and authority: and in respect thereof,

- 1\. Wisely maintain their authority.
- 2\. Rightly manage the same.

The well managing of their authority is #emph[generally] noted in this phrase, #emph[do the same things:] but more #emph[particularly] in #footnote[#emph[Col.] 4. 1.] another place referred to these two branches

- 1\. Justice.
- 2\. Equity.

Of these in order.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of masters choosing good servants.]

The first thing that a man, who taketh upon him to be a master, must take care of, is to entertain good servants into his service. #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 101. 6.] #emph[Mine eyes] (saith #emph[David) shall be upon the faithful of the land, that they may dwell with me: he that walketh in a perfect way he shall serve me.] What doth this phrase #emph[(mine eyes shall be upon the faithful)] imply, but that he will diligently and carefully inquire after such: yea #emph[mine eyes] (saith he) not another mans eyes: he would not put all the trust upon others: he would himself make what proof and trial he could. Such an emphasis hath this phrase #emph[(mine eyes)] where #footnote[#emph[Job] 19. 27.] #emph[Job] saith; #emph[whom I shall see for my self, and mine eyes shall behold, and not another for me.]

1. Servants are of all other things (except wife and children) #footnote[Reasons.] of best and greatest use. If then men be careful in well choosing other things, as houses, land, household-stuff, horses, and all manner of cattle (as men are very circumspect therein) should they not be much more careful in well choosing servants?

2. Thus will masters show that they seek, and aim at the good of their family: yea and in their family at the good of Church and common-wealth: for good members of a family are likeliest to prove profitable to Church & common-wealth.

3. When good servants are chosen, there is hope of receiving the more good from them, and doing more good unto them. They will be pliable to all good admonition, docible by all good instruction, serviceable in all things they take in hand.

#strong[#emph[Object.] A master may make a bad servant to become a good one.]

#emph[Answ.] There is a great hazard and venture therein, especially if they be grown to ripe years: no creature is easily tamed, or brought from the natural course it hath taken, after it is grown up: a bough that hath grown crooked many years together will hardly be made straight. It is not in mans power to make a bad servant good: neither can man expect Gods blessing therein, when he observeth not a right course warranted by God.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What course is fittest for choice of servants? #footnote[Direction for choosing of servants.]]

1. If they be young, see how they have been from their infancy educated. Great is the benefit of good education: and much good may be expected from thence: for it is the means which God hath sanctified for good.

But if they be grown to ripe years, choose such as fear God, such were they upon whom #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 101. 6.] #emph[David] cast his eyes.

On this ground did #footnote[#emph[Philem. vers.] 10.] Saint #emph[Paul] advise #emph[Philemon] to take #emph[Onesimus] again, because grace was then wrought in his heart.

1. Gods fear is the ground of all good obedience and #footnote[Why such servants as fear God are to be chosen.] faithfulness: note the examples of such good and faithful servants as are commended in Scripture, and you shall find them all to fear God.

2. Such will not only be diligent in their work, but they will also faithfully call upon God to prosper those things which they take in hand in their masters behalf, instance the example of #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 24. 12.] #emph[Abrahams] servant.

3. God will have such respect unto those servants which fear his name, as for their sakes to bless their masters house. Thus was #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 39. 5.] #emph[Potiphars] house blessed for #emph[Josephs] sake.

2. Make some trial of their fitness to that calling, work and service whereunto thou shalt put them. From such there may be hope of profit & benefit by their service. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 29. 14.] #emph[Laban] made trial of #emph[Jacob] a month before he covenanted for any long continuance.

3. Take such especially as are of mean and poor estate, and know not how to maintain themselves but by service. Thus will a double work of charity be done therein: and thus maist thou look for better service: for commonly such are most industrious, and most obedient to their masters.

#strong[#emph[Object.] When men have taken all the care they can in their choice, they may be deceived.]

#emph[Answ.] 1. Then much more likely is it, that if they be careless therein, they shall be deceived.

2. Diligent, and wise search, is a means to find out the disposition and ability of servants.

3. If having used the means men be deceived, they may have the more comfort in bearing the cross, because they have not wittingly or carelessly pulled it upon themselves.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of masters carelessness in choosing servants.]

Contrary is their carelessness, who are ready to receive any into their house, even Atheists, Papists, swearers, swaggerers, profane wretches, unclean persons, and such like, against whom #emph[David] protesteth, he would not have #emph[him that worketh deceit to dwell in] #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 101. 7.] #emph[his house; nor him that telleth lies to tarry in his sight.] They who entertain such, make their houses to be cages of unclean birds, seminaries of wicked persons, and unprofitable members: for one scabbed sheep is enough to infect a whole flock. No marvell though many mischiefs fall upon such a family: for a man were as good bring so many Snakes and Adders into his house, as such persons: yea with them is brought Gods curse. Can any good then be looked for? Such foolish masters regard neither themselves, nor their household: no nor yet Church or common-wealth. If none would entertain such, it might be a means to make them alter their condition.

Many are not only careless in choosing good servants, but also except against such as are religious; thinking it a matter of reproach to entertain them. By which conceit they oft put away Gods blessing from their house.

Others so stand upon a great portion of money with a prentice, or a clerk, or other servant, as they will take none but rich mens children. Whence it cometh to pass, that this point (which is a great point) of charity is neglected: persons most unfit for service are entertained, and such many times as scorn to do service. Thus their masters grow weary of them: the servants lose their time: never prove to be their crafts-masters: and their parents repent the giving of such a portion with them.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of masters maintaining their authority.]

After that masters have chosen good servants, their duty is well to use them: which by reason of the difference betwixt masters and servants cannot be well done, except masters wisely maintain their authority. A master therefore must be able #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 3. 4. τοῦ ἰδίου οἴκου προϊστάμενον εὖ.] #emph[well to rule his own house:] this is a duty which the Apostle in particular requireth of a Bishop, who is master of an house, but it appertaineth in general to all masters of families. Women also who by virtue of their places are mistresses, are commanded #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 14. οἰκοδεσποτεῖν.] #emph[to guide the house,] or to rule, and perform the part of a mistress therein. It was the #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 8. 9.] Centurions commendation, that having servants under him, he had them at his command.

1. Gods image and authority, which a master carrieth, is thus preserved.

2. Thus shall a master have much better service done. Not one servant of a thousand, that is not kept under authority, will do good service. A like duty to this was enjoined to an husband in relation to a wife, Treat. 4. §. 4. Some of the reasons, directions, & other points there handled, may be here applied. Read it therefore.

For this end three things are to be observed. #footnote[Direction.]

1. That masters carry themselves worthy of their place, and worthy of that honour which is due to them: which may best be done by making themselves a pattern of such good things as in their places appertain to them. #footnote[#emph[Jos.] 24. 15.] #emph[I and my house will fear the Lord,] saith #emph[Joshua:] he would not only put them to it, but he also would do it, he would go before them. #emph[I will behave my self wisely, I will walk within my house with a perfect heart,] saith #footnote[#emph[Psal.] 101. 2.] #emph[David.]

2. That masters keep their servants in awe and fear. #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 3. 4.] Children must be #emph[kept in subjection:] much more servants.

3. That masters do the things which they do in their carriage towards their servants with authority: #emph[Command, forbid, rebuke] (to use #footnote[#emph[Tit.] 2. 15.] the Apostles phrase) #emph[with all authority.] The manner of speech which the Centurion used to his servants (#footnote[#emph[Mat.] 8. 9.] #emph[go, come, do this)] savour of authority. So the Church taking upon her the person of a mistress, useth a word of authority, #footnote[#emph[Cant.] 3. 5.] #emph[I charge you] not to do this.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of masters making their authority to be despised.]

The aberrations in the defect contrary to the forenamed duty #footnote[How masters lose their honour.] and point of wisdom, are many: as

1. When masters carry themselves basely and abjectly before #footnote[1\. By baseness.] their servants: being light in their behaviour, foolish in their carriage, given to drunkenness, uncleanness, lewd company, and other vices. #emph[Nabal] was such an one: for his servant could say of him, #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 25. 17.] #emph[He is a son of Belial. David,] though he did not give himself over to such foul sins as these are, yet he oft failed in an unseemly carriage before his servants, as when #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 21. 13.] #emph[he changed his behaviour, and feigned himself mad, and scrabbled on doors, and let his spittle fall down upon his beard:] and again, #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 19. 3, 5.] when he gave such reins to his passion, for the death of his traitorous son #emph[Absalom,] as #emph[his people being ashamed stole away from him,] and #emph[Joab] his servant was forced roundly to tell him of it, saying, #emph[thou hast shamed this day the faces of all thy servants,] etc.

Mistresses oft lose their authority by conspiring with their servants to go abroad, take away goods, gossip, and do such other like things privily without their husbands consent: they make themselves thereby slaves to their servants, not daring to do any thing which may offend their servants, lest they should discover to their masters such lewd pranks as their mistresses did.

2. When masters are too remiss and sheepish: entreating and #footnote[2\. By remissness.] praying their servants to do such things as they ought to command and require at their hands: and if it be not done, all their remedy is patience, or else to do it themselves. Howsoever this might be counted meekness, and gentleness towards equals and strangers, over whom we have no authority, yet towards servants it is too base remissness: yea it is a relinquishing of that power which God hath given, and whereof God will take an account.

3. When masters suffer their servants to be their companions, #footnote[3\. By familiarity.] playing, drinking, revelling with them, and saying (as it is in the proverb) #emph[hail fellows met.] Thus servants oft take liberty to presume above their master: for men are naturally prone to ambition; and, #emph[if an inch be given, they will take an ell.] They who in this kind so far debase themselves, as to give their servants power over their own body, do make both themselves, and their true lawful bedfellow to be despised: #emph[themselves,] in that such servants as are so made one flesh, will think to keep in awe such a master or mistress, as they have known, through fear of revealing that sin: their #emph[bedfellow,] in that such servants will think to be maintained, and bolstered up by the master or mistress, whom they have so known. On this ground was #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 16. 3, 4.] #emph[Sarah] despised in the eyes of #emph[Hagar] her maid.

4. When masters are over-ruled by their servants to do any #footnote[4\. By letting go the reins.] unjust or unlawful thing: as #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 14. 17.] #emph[Joash,] who by his servants was drawn to idolatry: and #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 38. 5.] #emph[Zedekiah,] who gave the Prophet #emph[Jeremiah] into his servants hands, using this base and abject speech, #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 38. 5.] #emph[The King is not he that can do any thing against you.] Thus will servants soon prove masters: and if they once come to this high pitch to rule, rather then to be ruled, they will quickly prove intolerable. For #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 30. 22.] this is one of the four things noted by the Wiseman, which #emph[the earth cannot bear,] namely, #emph[a servant when he reigneth:] and #footnote[#emph[Eccl.] 10. 5, 7.] this is one of those evils which proceed from rulers, that #emph[servants ride upon horses,] that is, are advanced above their place and degree: whence it followeth, that #footnote[#emph[Eccl.] 10. 5, 7.] #emph[Governors walk as servants upon the earth,] they are dejected below their place and degree. It falleth out in this case betwixt servants and masters, as betwixt scales, or balances; if the weights that use to lie in one balance to keep it down be taken away, it will suddenly fly up, and so the other balance will be kept down.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of masters too great rigour.]

The contrary in the excess is too great rigour and austerity manifested in look, speech, and actions.

1. In #emph[look,] when a master cannot cast a good eye on his servant. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 2, 5.] #emph[Jacob] was much discountenanced by the countenance of his master. Many masters by their continual frowning brows, and fiery eyes, do much terrify their servants.

2. In #emph[speech,] when masters cannot give a good word to their servants, but if they be moved never so little, cast upon them all the reproachful names that they can call to mind: little thinking of this fearful doom, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 5. 22.] #emph[Whosoever shall say, thou fool, shall be in danger of hell fire.]

Thus did #emph[Saul] manifest a malicious and mischievous mind against #emph[Jonathan] and #emph[David] by the foul language he gave. Some have such a froward and perverse tongue, as they can never speak directly to their servants: but if they command or forbid a thing, they will do it after such a manner as their servant can scarce tell what they mean: and this not only when their servant hath offended them, but in their best mood. So shrewish are others, as their tongues seldom lie still: but they are ever chiding upon every small occasion: whereby it cometh to pass that their servants are no more moved with it, then the Doves, and Stares that continually abide in Belfries, are moved with the ringing of bells. Such bitterness also is mixed with the chidings of many, as they belch out of their black mouths, most direful imprecations. What can be said of such tongues, but that they are #footnote[#emph[Jam.] 3. 6.] #emph[set on fire of hell?] As other aberrations wherein masters are reproved, are to be applied to mistresses, so this especially. For mistresses do commonly most offend in shrewishness of speech.

3. In #emph[actions,] when masters are too frequent, and too furious #footnote[#emph[Quamuis dominus seruo praestet, tamen se aequalem iudicet quatenus homo existit. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12.] strikers: striking their servants on every occasion, not caring how they strike. In these and other like evidences of too much austerity and plain arrogancy masters forget that they are men. Though for outward order a master be more excellent then a servant, yet as a man he ought to judge himself equal. This extreme can be no good means to maintain authority: but it is a plain abuse thereof. Masters ought so #footnote[#emph[Serui colant potius te quàm timeant. Senec. epist.] 47.] to carry themselves as their servants may rather reverence, then dread them.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of masters commanding power restrained to things lawful.] #footnote[Extent of masters power.]

That a master may the better know how to maintain his authority, I will distinctly declare the #emph[extent] of his power, and that in these four particulars, 1. In #emph[Commanding.] 2. In #emph[Correcting.] 3. In #emph[ordering the marriage of their servants.] 4. In #emph[disposing their person.]

Within the lists of these two virtues #emph[Justice] and #emph[Equity] (whereunto all the duties which masters owe their servants shall #footnote[§. 20, 21, #emph[etc.]] afterward be referred) must the commanding power of masters be bounded.

- 1\. #emph[Justice] requireth two things,
  - 1\. A #emph[restraint] of masters commandments.
  - 2\. An #emph[execution] thereof.

The restraint is unto Gods law, that a master command nothing against it, but what is agreeable to it. #emph[Abishai] would #footnote[Nothing unlawful to be commanded by masters. 1 #emph[Sam.] 26. 9.] fain have had #emph[Davids] warrant to have killed #emph[Saul:] but #emph[David] was so far from commanding him to do it, as he kept him from it.

Masters are but subordinate ministers under God: they must therefore command nothing against his law. As a Judge, high-Sheriff, and all other officers under the King, must make the Kings law the ground of all those things which they require of the Kings subjects: so masters must make Gods law the ground of all those things which they require of their servants, who are also the servants of Christ. Besides, to what purpose is it to command that which a servant may and must refuse to do? But in no #footnote[Treat. 7. §. 38.] unlawful thing may he obey.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of masters presuming above their authority.]

Contrary is the arrogancy and presumption of many masters, who make their own will the rule of their servants obedience. This must needs be a leaden rule, which may be bowed this way, that way, and every way, because of the flexible and variable humour of man. Hence is it that many most vile and horrible things are commanded, because they are agreeable to the commanders humour. #emph[Absalom] bid his servants commit a most detestable murder upon his own brother, and note how he presseth it, #emph[Have not I commanded you?] #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 13. 28.]

More presumptuous is he that taketh upon him to be the #footnote[The Popes presumption.] great commander of all Christians, the Pope of Rome, and other masters and governors of Jesuits, Friers, Monks, and other like orders, who command such as are under their authority to commit treasons, raise rebellions, kill Kings, and do other like execrable villainies.

And to justify the Pope from whom all inferior governors receive their power, and by whose will they must be ruled, these blasphemous positions do Papists hold of his power, #emph[He can do all things of right as God. He is as God having] #footnote[#emph[Extravag. de major. & obed. Alban. in lib. de potestat. Pap. & Eccles. Gratian. in Decret. dist.] 40.] #emph[on earth fulness of power. If he shall draw with him innumerable souls of men into hell, yet none may say to him, what doest thou?] Doth not the Apostles #footnote[2 #emph[Thess.] 2. 4. #emph[In Ecclesia cath. semper creditum est Rom. Pontificem posse sua authoritate leges ferre, quae in conscientia obligent. Bellar. de Rom. Pontif. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 15.] description of #emph[that man of sin, the son of perdition,] agree to him of whom those things are spoken? Is he not in Papists account, #emph[as God, showing himself that he is God?] Which is further confirmed by that power which they give him of making laws to bind the conscience, and coining new articles of faith.

Not only popish, but profane masters also too much exceed in this presumptuous use of their power above their power: as they who enjoin their servants to kill, to steal, to swear, to forswear, to lie, to give false measures, and weights, to go to mass, to profane the Sabbath, with other like sins. In all these, and all other things like to these, being against Gods law, masters have no power to command: they go above their commission: and shall accordingly answer for it.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of masters commanding servants to do their duty.]

2. The execution of a masters commanding power consisteth in those things which God hath enjoined as bounden duties. These a master by virtue of his authority must command his servants to do: as to worship God, to sanctify his Sabbath, to be diligent and conscionable in his calling, with other like duties, which God compriseth under these words, #emph[the way of the Lord, righteousness and judgment:] and saith that he knew that #emph[Abram] would #emph[command his household to do them.] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 18. 19.]

The charge given to masters in the fourth commandment concerning servants, #emph[(thou nor thy man-servant, nor thy maidservant)] #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 20. 10.] proveth as much. #emph[Josiah] is commended for #emph[causing his] #footnote[2 #emph[Chr.] 34. 32.] #emph[people to stand to the covenant of God.] On this ground if a master have servants that are papists, separatists, or profane persons, he ought to command them, and cause them to go to the holy ordinances of God.

It is one of the principal ends why God hath given power and authority to some over others, that by their authority they may cause them which are under them to observe the commandments of God: even as inferior officers have authority given them, to see the Kings laws kept.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the sin of masters in suffering servants to neglect their duty.]

It is contrary to that commanding power which God hath given to masters, to suffer their servants to omit, and neglect those bounden duties which God hath commanded them: as if a servant be so popish or profane as to refuse to go to the word, or Sacrament, or to perform any duty whereunto he is bound, to let him alone. Though they themselves do those duties, and though they do not hinder and keep their servants from doing them, yet if they cause not their servants also to do them, they make themselves accessary to their servants sin.

#emph[Object.] Every mans conscience is free, and cannot be forced; therefore masters may not compel servants to such things as are against their conscience.

#emph[Answ.] Though the conscience be free to a man, and out of anothers power, yet their outward actions are not free: and though faith, piety, righteousness, nor any grace can be forced into men, yet they may be forced to use the means which God hath sanctified for the breeding and increasing of them. Though they cannot be forced to have a religious and righteous heart, yet they may be forced to do religious and righteous duties: or if a master cannot force these, yet he may and ought to do his uttermost endeavour: by which means though he cannot free his servant from death, yet he shall free his own soul from the guilt of his death.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of a masters wisdom in ordering things indifferent.]

Equity hath respect to those things which are in a masters power to command or not command: such are all civil actions as concern himself, concerning the particulars whereof God hath given no direct charge for an absolute performance of them: as to go of such an errand, to do such a message, to dispatch such a business. Concerning these things I may say of a masters power, as the Apostle doth of a parents power in #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 36, 37.] another case; If he seeth it meet to be done, and commandeth his servant to do it, #emph[he doth well, he sinneth not.] If he seeth it not meet to be done, and commandeth it not, #emph[he doth also well, he sinneth not.] Of this kind of things speaketh the Centurion, #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 8. 9.] where he saith, I say to one, #emph[go,] to another, #emph[come,] to a third, #emph[do this.]

The mark which masters must aim at in commanding or forbidding these, must be expediency: for #emph[all things that are] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 10. 23.] #emph[lawful, are not expedient.] Expediency dependeth much upon circumstances, and consequences which may follow upon the doing of any thing: in observing whereof the wisdom of him who hath power to have a thing done, or not done, especially appeareth. When #emph[David] suffered not #emph[Hushai] his good friend, #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 15. 33, 34.] and wise counsellor, to go with him when he fled from #emph[Absalom,] but bid him return to the city and there abide, he had an eye to the good consequence that might follow thereupon. And when #emph[Joab] commanded #emph[Cushi] rather then #emph[Ahimaaz] to #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 18. 20, 21.] carry the news of #emph[Absoloms] death, he had an eye to the meetness of the persons, and to the kind of message.

To apply this to our purpose, #emph[Equity] requireth that masters (in commanding things indifferent, which they have power to command or not to command) have an eye to their servants ability, sex, age, disposition, conscience, and other like circumstances, that the thing which they command be somewhat agreeable to them, not above their ability, not unbeseeming their sex, not unfitting their age, not thwarting their disposition, not against their conscience.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of masters offence against expediency.]

It is contrary to equity for a master to regard only himself, and his own mind, even in things that are lawful. #emph[Paul] was not #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 10. 33.] of this mind, when he said, #emph[He sought not his own profit:] no nor his own will. Expediency and inexpediency were great motives unto him, to forbear things which were otherwise lawful: yet little is this regarded by many: for

1. Many command things to the very uttermost of their servants strength, if not above it, (as #emph[Pharaoh)] or else things dangerous, #footnote[#emph[Exod.] 5. 7.] which may bring much mischief upon them. #emph[David] #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 23. 17.] was touched in heart for moving only by a wish his servants to fetch him water with the danger of their lives.

2. Others against comeliness put men to do maids works, and maids to do mens work.

3. Others upon mere partiality keep under old and good servants, and prefer much younger before them.

4. Others will forcibly make servants do things against their natural disposition, as to handle, yea and eat such things as they cannot endure to touch or see.

5. Others in doubtful matters will urge and press them against their conscience.

Howsoever in these and other such like things not unlawful, servants ought to do what they can to satisfy their masters command, if he be peremptory in urging them: yet ought not a master to be too peremptory in pressing his authority and power.
